 GitOps vs Infrastructure as Code (Terraform + GitOps)

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring an important comparison: GitOps vs Infrastructure as Code (IaC) and how tools like Terraform and GitOps work together in modern DevOps environments. 🚀
Many people confuse these concepts, but they actually complement each other.

🔹 Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
 Infrastructure as Code focuses on provisioning and managing infrastructure using code.
For example, with Terraform we can define resources such as:
Virtual machines
Networks
Load balancers
Cloud services
These configurations allow infrastructure to be automated, version-controlled, and reproducible.

🔹 GitOps
 GitOps focuses on deploying and managing applications and configurations using Git as the source of truth.
GitOps tools monitor Git repositories and automatically apply changes to the running system, often within Kubernetes clusters.

🔹 Key Differences
Infrastructure as Code:
Used to create and manage infrastructure resources
GitOps:
Used to deploy and manage applications and configurations

🔹 How Terraform and GitOps Work Together
A common modern workflow looks like this:
Terraform → Creates cloud infrastructure
 Kubernetes → Runs applications
 GitOps Tool → Deploys and manages app configurations
In this setup:
Terraform provisions the infrastructure
GitOps manages application deployment and configuration updates

💡 Key Insight:
 IaC builds the foundation infrastructure, while GitOps ensures applications are continuously deployed and synchronized with Git.
